.TH UUDECODE 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
uudecode \- decode a uuencoded file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uudecode
.RB [ \-o
.IR outfile ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uudecode
reads
.I file
(or standard input, if it is omitted), which is expected to have been
produced by
.BR uuencode (1),
and recreates the original file, including its permission mode.
.PP
Any lines before the
.B "begin mode filename"
header line are skipped without complaint (historical
.B uudecode
has always tolerated leading mail-transport headers this way); the
first line that begins with
.B "begin "
is treated as the real header, and its absence anywhere in the input
is a diagnosed error. The
.I filename
field of the header line may itself contain spaces, since only the
.I mode
field before it is whitespace-delimited.
.PP
Decoding stops at the encoded data's own zero-length terminator line,
which must be immediately followed by a line containing exactly
.BR end .
Anything else there, including reaching the end of the input first, is
a diagnosed, truncated-stream error rather than being treated as an
implicit terminator. Likewise, every character read as encoded data is
checked against the encoding's own alphabet before being decoded; an
invalid character anywhere in the data is a diagnosed error as soon as
it is seen.
.PP
The recreated file's pathname is the header line's
.I filename
field, unless overridden by
.BR \-o .
No sanitization is applied to that pathname, whether it came from the
header or from
.BR \-o :
whatever names a creatable, writable path on this platform is used
exactly as given.
.PP
The recreated file's permission mode is always set to the mode
recorded in the header line (masked to the low 12 permission bits),
even when
.B \-o
overrides the destination filename: only
.I where
the file is written changes, not what mode it is given.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " outfile"
Write the decoded data to
.I outfile
instead of the pathname recorded in the header line. The permission
mode recorded in the header is still applied to
.I outfile
(see above).
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
The uuencoded input to read. If omitted, standard input is read
instead.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The file was decoded and recreated successfully, including its
permission mode.
.TP
.B 1
.I file
could not be opened or contained no valid
.B begin
line, the encoded stream was truncated, malformed, or contained an
invalid character, the output file could not be created or written,
or (a separate failure that can occur even after the file's data was
otherwise written successfully) its permission mode could not be
changed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR uuencode (1)
